nicki morgan we learned this morning  that this man wayne cousins  was assigned to the house of commons to  guard  mps  of which you would have been one um  what's your reaction to discovering that  well good morning trevor um well my  reaction to to that actually is that um  of course it's a it's a development in  the story but actually this is not about  parliament this is not about um the  guarding of uh of parliament there's  always an issue about vetting i know  you've been talking to other guests  about that but i really hope we don't  make this about parliament um because  actually this is about the safety of  women and girls across the country many  of whom don't work in a very protective  place like parliament and westminster  and many of whom do uh exactly what  sarah everard does and they did and they  they do it every day which is to walk  somewhere home decide perhaps not to get  a bus to go for a run  um and if they were stopped by a police  officer perhaps until last week they  would have done what they were asked to  do um now of course that relationship of  trust between the police and i think  many women and girls in this country is  teetering on a knife edge and there's a  a lot of work for the police uh to do  and i think we need to concentrate on  that rather than this development about  parliament it may well turn out he's  guarded other places too we just don't  know but actually it's about the safety  of women and girls like sarah everard  palm sanders just suggested that every  police officer  should be  re-vetted  would you agree with that  well i would agree with that um and i  think as your other some of your other  guests have said already this is um a  much broader cultural issue i mean it's  kind of inevitable that we are this  morning talking about whether it's  vetting  or we're talking about how exactly uh  wayne cousins got to the position that  he was in was able to move forces and of  course that has got to be looked at but  there's also a broader cultural issue i  think what palm sound is just saying  it's not it's in it's in all workplaces  actually it's in  all organizations and it's on our  streets as well whereby it is just  appalling and unacceptable that women  and girls do not feel safe when they are  walking around often in broad daylight  that's a cultural issue and that's a  cultural issue that the police have  absolutely got to get to grips with and  frankly that's the test for cressida  dick you know  i'd like to hear from her a plan about  how she is going to tackle  changing the culture in the met and  actually from all the other potential  chief constables as well now at that  point that's the test for whether she  keeps the job or not  let's address this wider cultural issue  one aspect of this is who should be  responsible for  fixing the problem as it were well we've  heard  the reaction for example to the  met advice that women should wave down a  bus i don't think we need to detain  ourselves on that but more  importantly i suppose i talked a little  earlier on to party chairman about the  remarks of the north yorkshire  police and crime commissioner who said  that women need to be streetwise about  when they can be arrested and when they  can't be arrested  oliver dowden said he and the prime  minister were outraged but i don't think  they were outraged enough to say this  person should be should not be a member  of the conservative party what do you  think  well i mean i have to say i think they  were incredibly stupid remarks um and  they were delivered by somebody who  clearly has never been in a situation  where they have walked down a street and  heard footsteps behind them and wondered  uh you know what's going to happen in  the next couple of minutes  uh got the keys out their bags in order  to be able to uh to defend themselves if  they if they have to um and frankly for  a police and crime commissioner to  basically hint that actually um women  need to be more uh aware um about uh  more skeptical actually if a police  officer stops them um i think is is  frankly uh extraordinary  um i mean i have to i'm not in favor i  mean i hope that um that philip outlet  has been uh contacted by whether it's  the party chairman whether it's the home  secretary whether it's the prime  minister and asked to explain himself um  and you know again at the end of the day  if somebody senior has said something or  not done something i would rather they  actually admitted a mayor cooper i've  got this wrong here's my plan for doing  it  rather than you know throwing people out  of the party or or getting them to  resign uh imminently so i think we  better hear from mr allen what his plan  is and if i were a woman in his police  and uh a crime kushner area then i would  want to hear from him what his plan is  actually make women feel safer when  they're going about their lives  but i suppose what i'm really uh trying  to get at here is that this is an  example of what i think you would say  you would describe as part of the  cultural problem that actually  the the big issue andy burnham talked  about this uh uh a moment ago the big  issue here is  not that women need to fix this but  there needs to be something uh about the  way that men behave now one of the  things we've heard about in this week is  the locker room  culture  that female police officers describe  one of the reasons that you left the  house of commons is that  you said that  you got lots of abuse and we know that  women mps are more likely to get abuse  uh and be undermined than male mps do  you think we can do anything about this  kind of culture  uh yeah i i do think we can do something  uh about it um and i know that there is  a huge determination amongst uh  ministers amongst the uh speaker of the  house of commons for example to tackle  um online abuse of members of parliament  but also uh actually the the new  elections bill proposes um  makes proposals about the intimidation  of candidates for example but you're  absolutely right trevor this is a wider  broader problem that has gone un-um  untested for or unchallenged for far too  long and of course as well as being uh  minister for women equalities a few  years ago i was also education secretary  and i heard what andy burnham said and i  think he is correct to say that actually  this is um also a challenge for men to  to call out when they hear unacceptable  language or so-called banter being used  but actually it starts right away at  schools and  the pshe curriculum the uh is so  important in what it teaches uh both  boys and girls about respect for  themselves about about issues about uh  consent about the language that we use  um you know when i was education  secretary i talked a lot about character  that's also about thinking about the  impact that you're having on people uh  around you wanting to do the right thing  even when somebody is not looking all of  that and then of course oliver dowden  also rightly highlighted  and again as a former culture secretary  the impact that the online harms bill  will need to have about making sure  because sadly a lot of abuse i suffered  and others suffer i think is absolutely  fueled by the misogyny that is allowed  to go rampant on our social media  platforms  actually that bill will be very  important again in beginning to change  the culture of those platforms make  those tech companies accountable for  what's on there getting that content off  and saying being transparent about how  they do that and of course policing that  to make sure they are doing what they  say they're going to to protect the  users of those platforms  indeed just very briefly of course  at the center of this is still the issue  of  uh the way that  women are spoken about and regard and so  on and this is a non-partisan point when  harriet harman was in government  there were things said about her  you know obsessed by women's issues and  so on and we we see to some extent  the  treatment of uh liz truss and pretty  patel  do you think that actually  there is a problem in  politics that  prominent women are not spoken about or  regarded in the same way as prominent  men  i don't think it's universal but i think  a lot of the coverage  of women in politics still focuses far  too much on obviously um what we are uh  how we are presenting rather than what  we are actually saying i mean i think we  remember that picture do you remember of  theresa may nicolas sturgeon when they  first met and that you know that picture  of them sitting there and i think was  more commentary about their knees being  on show than what they actually said in  their meeting and of course i remember  walking down downing street um and  somewhere there's a very unattractive  picture of me  and uh because i was walking down  without my rod and the photographer  shouted out girls girls and i was like  no girls we are secretaries of state and  i think absolutely the language that  people use as i say about all of us um  people in public life generally  obviously matters and again we all have  to think about that at at all times it  is much better than i think it it was uh  but there's always room to prove not  just in westminster in all organizations  and of course the spotlight is very  firmly on our police forces at the  moment
 

